 ### Interface hook sets
 
 Ethernet interfaces use the `_l2` programs; interfaces without an Ethernet header use `_l3`. Bridge and bond slaves receive equivalent owned hooks because traffic may bypass a master qdisc.
+
+This F50 build has a narrow SIPA WAN adapter: `sipa_eth0` must report RAWIP
+(`type=519`), six address bytes, and expose both `/sys/module/sipa_eth` and
+`/sys/kernel/debug/sipa_eth/sipa_eth0`. It selects `wan_egress_l2` because locally
+originated SIPA traffic retains Ethernet framing. `wan_ingress_sipa` accepts the
+driver's exact pseudo-Ethernet MAC pair (`16:15:14:13:12:11` destination,
+`11:12:13:14:15:16` source) and matching IPv4/IPv6 EtherType/version. It also accepts
+protocol-matching RAWIP replies: Linux strips the MAC when `dae0_ingress`
+redirects a reply into this RAWIP device. Other IP framing is rejected; non-IP
+protocols retain the ordinary pass-through behavior. Normal IP/transport
+validation and conntrack processing remain shared with the WAN ingress path.
+Missing or inconsistent SIPA identity evidence fails attachment explicitly.
+Startup and interface rebind use the same selector and log the selected SIPA
+programs. Other interface names retain the existing L2/L3 choice; this does not
+reclassify all RAWIP devices or change the kernel interface type.
 
 | Topology | LAN-side hooks | WAN-side hooks |
 | --- | --- | --- |
